implementationPlan.description:
THE DIRECT ARMS, RED FIRST. NEW `tests/e2e/control-room/lane-landing.test.ts` — root vitest picks it up (`vitest.config.ts` include carries `tests/**/*.test.ts`), so it runs under `pnpm test` with no config change. Siblings for style and size: `wrapper-lane.test.ts` (167) and `daemon-children.test.ts` (144).

These arms exercise the GENERATED SEAT CHILD DIRECTLY — spawn the `.js` the double writes, feed it a mission on stdin, and assert what it did to disk. That is the seam where the bug lives, and it runs in seconds instead of minutes.

FEED THE ARMS REAL MISSION TEXT, not a hand-written approximation. Import `codeMission` and `mission` from `apps/daemon/src/orchestrator/agent-mission-text.js` (lane-landing.ts already reaches into apps/daemon with a relative import, so the precedent and the path shape exist). The discriminator is exact and measured: `codeMission` :110 opens `You are a moe-next coding agent. You hold the durable claim on code node "<nodeRef>"`; `mission` :294 opens `You are a moe-next agent. You hold the durable claim on work item "<workItemId>" (command kind <kind>)`. Match on the code-node clause carrying THE TARGET REF — never an arbitrary substring of the instructions, which would match any mission that happened to quote a ref.

CASES, each asserting a specific outcome and not merely 'did not crash':
- the TARGET node's `codeMission` writes the file, with the exact expected bytes;
- `mission(workItemId, 'policy.validate', ...)` writes NOTHING — assert the file is still absent, and separately that a pre-existing file is left BYTE-IDENTICAL (a policy seat arriving AFTER the code seat must not touch it);
- a DIFFERENT code node's `codeMission` writes nothing;
- EMPTY stdin, and stdin closed with no write, both write nothing and exit 0;
- REPEAT invocation with the target mission is BYTE-STABLE: same bytes, no truncation, no timestamp drift. Assert equality of the file's bytes across two runs — this is the arm that pins the SUBMITTED_BYTES-digest failure;
- CONCURRENT invocation (target and policy at once) leaves exactly the target's bytes;
- foreign bytes elsewhere in the workspace survive every case.

Assert the child's EXIT STATUS too, and assert a genuine IO failure surfaces rather than being swallowed. Per the global rail, a test asserting only 'it refused' is one layer from vacuous: pin what was written, what was not, and the exit code.

These arms are RED at this step. Do not edit lane-landing.ts yet.

THE COMMITTED REGRESSION (DoD 1). NEW `tests/e2e/control-room/lane-landing.spec.ts` — the standalone real-lane Playwright arm, the permanent form of step 1's throwaway. Delete the throwaway now if it is still on disk.

N INDEPENDENT FRESH SEEDED LANES, per the owner ruling — each iteration its own `withDaemonBackedControlRoom({liveCredentials:'ABSENT', fakeGh:'SUCCESS'})` and its own `landLaneNode`, never a second call against a lane that already landed. Pick N from step 1's measured failure rate so the arm would have failed before the fix, and say in a comment WHY that N: an intermittent arm with N too small is green by luck and pins nothing.

ASSERT, PER LANE:
- `landed.ok` is true, with the refusal detail in the failure message so a red is diagnosable;
- the returned `sha` DIFFERS from the pre-landing baseline sha AND EQUALS the workspace's actual `git rev-parse HEAD` — read from git, never parsed from the transcript;
- the TARGET node carries a COMMITTED landing receipt, read through the measured production path `readReviewLedgers(store, projectId, new Set([nodeRef])).landings` (`apps/daemon/src/review/review-read-model.ts:288`). Never credit a `[lander]` log line as the receipt, and never credit a policy line at all;
- `git status --porcelain` in the lane workspace is EMPTY after the helper's teardown;
- the wrapper pid returned in `LaneLanded.wrapperPid` is GONE — no surviving owned process.

DO NOT DELETE OR RESET DIRTY BYTES TO MAKE THE CLEAN-STATUS ASSERTION PASS (owner fence). If the workspace is dirty, that is the bug and the arm must fail.

TEARDOWN in a `finally` on every path including the throwing one: the lane, the daemon child, the wrapper, any temp root. A leaked wrapper keeps staffing against a store the lane is about to delete, and a leaked process makes every later gate on this board inadmissible.

DO NOT TOUCH THE PEER'S FILES. `contract-goal.spec.ts`, `lane-contract-goal.ts` and `lane-multi-node-workspace.ts` are untracked in the shared worktree and belong to task-6bafd3b9 (worker-76f4ef35). Do not sweep, revert, commit or import them, and do not create a dependency cycle waiting on that consumer.

comments.content:
MEASURED before our contract graph is created: contract-goal.spec.ts calls createLaneContractGoal; its first effect after resolving scratch is await landLaneNode(lane). No prepared test files, graph seal, new seat or criterion service has run when the following failure occurs. Latest command `pnpm exec playwright test -c tests/e2e/control-room/playwright.config.ts contract-goal.spec.ts` -> 1 passed (workspace-only arm), 1 failed, 3.3m, exit 1. Assertion at lane-contract-goal.ts:73: legacy.ok false; detail LANDING_BUDGET_SPENT followed by repeated `[wrapper] policy.validate@e2e-proj-qknzux-policy: STAFFING_ATTEMPTS_EXHAUSTED`. Earlier run reached our next wrapper, which refused both producer nodes with BASELINE_WORKSPACE_DIRTY / REPOSITORY_DELIVERY_BASELINE_UNAVAILABLE (REPOSITORY_DELIVERY). That earlier run did not capture which dirty path, so the common-cause restaff/write race is a HYPOTHESIS, not established causation. Another run got past legacy landing with a clean workspace; failure is intermittent.

Minimal standalone REPRO SOURCE (new temporary Playwright spec next to lane-landing.ts; not yet independently run as this reduced source):
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';
import { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process';
import { withDaemonBackedControlRoom } from './daemon-ports.js';
import { landLaneNode } from './lane-landing.js';
test('legacy landing returns a clean baseline', async () => {
  test.setTimeout(240_000);
  const result = await withDaemonBackedControlRoom({liveCredentials:'ABSENT', fakeGh:'SUCCESS'}, async lane => {
    const landed = await landLaneNode(lane);
    expect(landed.ok, JSON.stringify(landed)).toBe(true);
    expect(execFileSync('git', ['status','--porcelain'], {cwd:lane.workspace, encoding:'utf8'})).toBe('');
  });
  expect(result.ok, JSON.stringify(result)).toBe(true);
});

Source evidence to investigate, not proof of root cause: landingSeatDouble ignores mission/arguments and always writes timestamped LANDED_PATH; landLaneNode uses LANDING_MAX_AGENTS=2, continuous wrapper, notices a file and submits only one round, kills only after first COMMITTED line. Policy-only seats therefore can write the same file as the code seat. No fixes made to shared lane modules.
